window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["raptor"]=[{"id":"raptor-front-bumper","title":"Aftermarket Front Bumper for the Gen 1 Raptor","vehicle":"raptor","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":699,"high":1599},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","penetrating oil","wire cutters (fog light wiring)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wiring basics"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2014"],"tags":["bumper","armor","winch-mount","front-end","recovery"],"parts":[{"name":"ADD Offroad Raptor Series Front Bumper \u2014 Gen 1","vendor":"Addictive Desert Designs","price_estimate_usd":1099},{"name":"ADD Offroad Stealth Fighter Front Bumper \u2014 Gen 1 Raptor","vendor":"Addictive Desert Designs","price_estimate_usd":1499},{"name":"Ranch Hand Legend Front Bumper \u2014 Gen 1 Raptor","vendor":"Ranch Hand","price_estimate_usd":799},{"name":"Fab Fours Premium Front Bumper \u2014 Gen 1 Raptor","vendor":"Fab Fours","price_estimate_usd":1199},{"name":"Rigid Industries Front Bumper \u2014 Gen 1 Raptor","vendor":"Rigid Industries","price_estimate_usd":1299}],"citations":[{"title":"Gen 1 vs Gen 2 Raptor bumper fitment differences \u2014 RaptorForum","url":"https://www.raptorforum.com/threads/gen-1-vs-gen-2-bumper-fitment.html"},{"title":"Addictive Desert Designs Gen 1 Raptor bumper install guide","url":"https://www.addfab.com/pages/gen1-raptor-bumper-install"}],"alternatives":["raptor-winch"],"summary":"The factory Gen 1 Raptor front fascia looks aggressive but is plastic over foam \u2014 an aftermarket steel bumper is the prerequisite for a winch mount, proper D-ring recovery points, and the approach angle the truck's suspension is capable of clearing.","description":"The Gen 1 Raptor's factory front end is a styled piece designed to complement the truck's appearance and meet pedestrian safety regulations \u2014 it is not a recovery or protection component. The plastic lower chin and the tow hooks molded into the bumper cover are not rated for serious recovery loads. Before you buy a winch, the first question is whether you have a front bumper that can carry it. The factory setup cannot.\n\nAddictive Desert Designs (ADD) is the most widely recommended brand for Gen 1 Raptor bumpers, largely because they're built in Tempe, Arizona specifically for desert running use cases \u2014 which aligns well with what the Raptor was designed to do. Their base Raptor Series bumper at $1,099 is a direct-bolt replacement in 3/16\" laser-cut steel with a winch cradle and two D-ring mounts. The Stealth Fighter at $1,499 adds a more aggressive approach angle cut and lighting provisions. Both are Gen 1 specific \u2014 the front frame horns and mounting points on the 2010\u20132014 truck are different from the 2017+ Gen 2, and bumpers are not interchangeable. Confirm the listing says Gen 1 or 2010\u20132014 before ordering.\n\nRanch Hand's Legend bumper is the value choice at $799 \u2014 heavier than the ADD units (Ranch Hand builds to grille guard standards), which may be a tradeoff on a truck you're running at speed. Fab Fours and Rigid Industries split the difference in price and style, with Rigid's bumper designed around their own light bar integration. All of these require disabling or rerouting the factory fog lights, which mount in the factory fascia \u2014 most aftermarket bumpers include provisions for aftermarket fogs but not the stock units.","steps":["Disconnect the negative battery terminal before any wiring work.","Remove the factory front fascia: pull the plastic clips along the top edge, remove the two lower corner bolts, and disconnect the fog light wiring harness connectors.","Remove the factory tow hooks (two bolts each, may be corroded \u2014 soak with penetrating oil the night before).","Remove the factory crash bar if present (two frame rail bolts per side). Set aside or discard \u2014 aftermarket bumpers mount directly to the frame horns.","Dry-fit the new bumper with assistance (these weigh 90\u2013150 lbs). Confirm the bumper mounts align with the frame holes; some fitment variation exists between early and late Gen 1 production years.","Torque mounting bolts to manufacturer spec (typically 75\u2013100 ft-lb depending on bolt size and manufacturer).","Route and reconnect fog light wiring per the bumper manufacturer's instructions. Most ADD bumpers use the same wiring harness connector as the factory lights if you're running ADD-compatible fogs.","Reconnect the battery and confirm fog lights function. Check bumper clearance against the hood and fenders before driving."],"pros":["Adds proper, rated D-ring recovery points at the front of the truck","Creates a winch cradle \u2014 required before mounting any 12,000+ lb winch","3/16\" or 1/4\" steel provides genuine rock and brush protection that the plastic fascia cannot","Improves approach angle on aggressive downhill entries","Improves the front-end appearance significantly for most buyers"],"cons":["Adds 90\u2013150 lbs to the front end, which affects the front suspension tune slightly and may require revalving at the extreme","Factory fog lights require replacement with aftermarket units compatible with the new bumper","Gen 1 fitment is specific \u2014 double-check the model year before ordering, especially for bumpers marketed as \"Raptor\" without a generation designation","Expensive for what is structurally a bolt-on bracket; budget over $1,000 for a quality unit"]},{"id":"raptor-skid-plates","title":"Skid Plate Kit for the Gen 1 Raptor \u2014 Oil Pan, Transfer Case, and Fuel Tank","vehicle":"raptor","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":399,"high":999},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","undercarriage work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2014"],"tags":["skid-plates","armor","underbody","protection","oil-pan","fuel-tank"],"parts":[{"name":"ADD Offroad Gen 1 Raptor Skid Plate Package","vendor":"Addictive Desert Designs","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"Zbroz Racing Gen 1 Raptor Full Skid Kit","vendor":"Zbroz Racing","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"DRC Fabrication Gen 1 Raptor Skid Kit","vendor":"DRC Fabrication","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"APS Gen 1 Raptor Full Underbody Skid System","vendor":"APS","price_estimate_usd":599}],"citations":[{"title":"Gen 1 Raptor underbody exposure points \u2014 RaptorForum tech discussion","url":"https://www.raptorforum.com/threads/skid-plates-underbody-protection-gen1.html"},{"title":"3/16 vs 1/4 inch skid plate thickness comparison \u2014 Expedition Portal","url":"https://expeditionportal.com/forum/threads/skid-plate-thickness-316-vs-14.html"}],"alternatives":["raptor-front-bumper"],"summary":"The Gen 1 Raptor's IFS underbody is exposed in ways a solid-axle rig's is not \u2014 at desert speeds over unexpected rocks and ledges, the oil pan, differential, and transfer case are directly in the strike zone, and skid plates are the lowest-cost way to protect the most expensive components.","description":"The factory Raptor ships with a light plastic under-tray that handles road debris but is not a structural skid plate. At speed across the kind of terrain the Raptor's suspension invites \u2014 high desert washes, rocky ledges, fast trail crossings \u2014 the plastic deflects instead of protecting. The IFS geometry puts the oil pan, lower differential housing, and front driveshaft lower than you'd expect for a truck with 9\" of suspension travel. When the suspension is at full compression over a ledge, those components are well within strike distance.\n\nThe key areas covered by a complete skid kit for the Gen 1 Raptor are: the oil pan (the most expensive single-strike repair at $600\u2013$1,200 for the 6.2L), the front differential housing, the transfer case (NV273 on the Gen 1), and the fuel tank. Most full kits cover all four; some entry-level kits are oil pan and diff only. Budget accordingly \u2014 a partial kit that skips the fuel tank is saving the wrong money if you're running the truck on rocky terrain.\n\nThe 3/16\" vs 1/4\" steel question is about use case. 3/16\" (approximately 4.8mm) is adequate for most trail and overland use \u2014 it absorbs a direct rock strike on a moving vehicle without deforming and bending into the component it's protecting. 1/4\" is for rock crawling and repeated high-force impacts; on a Raptor, which is not a crawling platform, 3/16\" is the practical choice and saves significant weight (a full 1/4\" skid kit adds 60\u201380 lbs over a 3/16\" equivalent). ADD and Zbroz both offer laser-cut 3/16\" kits with proper mounting provisions; Zbroz's higher price point reflects thicker plate and more coverage area on their full kit.\n\nThe mounting system matters: a skid that uses existing frame bolts drops in without drilling, which is the right design. Avoid kits that require welding or that use the oil pan drain bolt as a mounting point \u2014 the latter complicates oil changes every time.","steps":["Lift the front of the truck and support on jack stands at the frame rails. Do not jack under the IFS control arms.","Clean the mounting areas on the frame with a wire brush. Apply penetrating oil to any factory undertray bolts that are corroded \u2014 on trucks that have seen salt or mud, these are routinely seized.","Remove the factory plastic under-tray (typically 8\u201312 push-pins and two bolts). Discard or keep as a liner under the skid for rock debris management.","Dry-fit the skid plate sections before committing to torque. Verify clearance around the drain plug, differential fill plug, and any underside inspection points.","Install skids using the provided hardware. Torque to manufacturer spec \u2014 typically 35\u201345 ft-lb for 3/8\" grade-8 mounting bolts. Over-torquing into aluminum frame threads is a common mistake; use a torque wrench.","Confirm the oil drain plug is still accessible with the skid in place. Some designs require a short extension to reach it; a sealed square drain plug (like Fumoto) streamlines this.","Drive over a moderate obstacle and inspect the skid for deformation or loosened hardware after the first run."],"pros":["Protects the most expensive single-impact repair items (oil pan, differential) on a truck designed to run fast over rough terrain","Bolt-on installation in 2\u20134 hours with basic tools","Steel skids stay in place after a strike, unlike plastic which deflects and can bend into components","Full kits cover fuel tank \u2014 critical on high-centering scenarios where the tank is the first contact point"],"cons":["Adds 40\u201380 lbs of unsprung and frame-mounted weight depending on kit thickness and coverage","Access to drain plugs and fill ports requires removal on some kit designs \u2014 choose a kit with access provisions","Gen 1 Raptor IFS means the front skids have limited clearance; the skid plates reduce ground clearance slightly (~0.5\u20131\") by hanging below frame height","Aftermarket fitment varies \u2014 some kits require minor modification on late 2014 models with production changes"]},{"id":"gen3-37-package-vs-non-37","title":"Gen 3 Raptor 37 Package \u2014 Is the $7,500 Option Worth It?","vehicle":"raptor","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"factory_options","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":7500},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-present"],"tags":["37-package","gen3","tires","gears","brakes","brembo","4.10","factory"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich KO2 37x12.5R17 (individual \u2014 factory pricing as option)","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":390},{"name":"4.10 axle gear set (aftermarket equivalent)","vendor":"Ford Racing","price_estimate_usd":1800}],"citations":[{"title":"Ford F-150 Raptor Gen 3 37 Package factory specs","url":"https://media.ford.com"},{"title":"Gen 3 Raptor option pricing MSRP","url":"https://www.ford.com/trucks/f150/raptor/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Gen 3 Raptor 37 Package includes 37x12.5R17 BFG KO2s, 4.10 axle gears, and Brembo brakes \u2014 not bigger tires. If you want 37s on a Gen 3 and don't have the package, you're missing the gear ratio that makes them functional. The package costs more than adding the tires alone post-purchase.","description":"The $7,500 (approximate MSRP) for the 37 Package is straightforward to frame as paying $7,500 for 37-inch tires. That framing is wrong. The tires are part of the package. The more important parts are the 4.10 axle gears and the Brembo brake upgrade that come with them.\n\n- **37x12.5R17 BFGoodrich KO2 tires** \u2014 four of them plus a matching spare (five total)\n- **17x8.5 forged aluminum beadlock-capable wheels** \u2014 upgraded from the base Gen 3 forged wheels\n- **4.10 axle gears front and rear** \u2014 the base Gen 3 runs 3.73 gears\n- **Upgraded Brembo front brake calipers and larger rotors** \u2014 the 37s add unsprung weight, the Brembos restore braking performance\n- **Fox Live Valve shock tune** \u2014 recalibrated for the 37s' additional weight\n\nThe base Gen 3 Raptor runs 3.73 axle gears. The 3.5L EcoBoost at 450 hp can push the truck through any situation with 3.73s, but the experience with 37s on 3.73s is noticeably worse than 35s on 3.73s. Acceleration from a stop is sluggish relative to what the engine should be capable of, highway RPM rises, and the transmission hunts more between gears.\n\nThe 4.10 gears in the 37 Package fix this. They're the correct calibration for 37-inch tires \u2014 the truck drives like a Raptor again. Everything Ford adjusted in the 37 Package (shock tune, gears, brakes) reflects the fact that 37s are not aesthetics \u2014 they change the truck's dynamic character in ways that require engineering responses.\n\nIf you buy a base Gen 3 and later want 37s with the right gear ratio, the cost calculation changes:\n\n- Four 37x12.5R17 BFG KO2s (replacing the stock 315/70R17s): ~$1,600 for tires\n- 4.10 ring and pinion front and rear plus installation: $2,500\u2013$4,000\n- Brembo brake upgrade (if desired): $1,800\u2013$3,000\n- Fox shock retune for 37s: $400\u2013$800\n- **Total post-purchase cost to reach 37 Package spec:** $6,300\u2013$9,800\n\nAt that range, the $7,500 factory option is not expensive \u2014 it's integrated, warrantied, and calibrated by engineers who knew what they were doing. The factory option also means the truck is covered under Ford's warranty in the 37 Package configuration. Dealer-installed gear sets may void powertrain coverage, depending on the dealership and the tech.\n\n- Anyone planning to run the truck primarily on-road \u2014 the 37s add rolling resistance, reduce fuel economy (about 1\u20132 MPG vs. stock 315/70), and increase highway noise\n- Anyone planning to upgrade to aftermarket suspension and custom gear ratios regardless \u2014 if you're building the truck heavily, the factory package becomes a baseline you'll modify anyway\n- Budget-constrained buyers \u2014 the $7,500 adds significantly to purchase price. A Gen 3 base with 315/70R17 is still a capable truck\n\n- Anyone planning serious desert or trail use with 37s \u2014 the gear ratio integration alone justifies it\n- Anyone who won't be doing significant aftermarket suspension modification \u2014 the factory package is properly engineered\n- Anyone who values factory warranty coverage on the drivetrain","steps":["Confirm via window sticker or build sheet whether a specific Gen 3 has the 37 Package","If buying new: run the math on post-purchase upgrade cost vs. factory option price","If buying used with 37 Package: verify the Brembo calipers are present (red, with \"Brembo\" cast in) and the 4.10 gear ratio (oil fill tag on the differential may indicate gear ratio; verify via vehicle order sheet)","If buying used without 37 Package and planning to add 37s: budget $6,000+ for proper gear ratio integration"],"pros":["Factory integration means all components are calibrated together","Covered under Ford's factory warranty","Five matching tires including spare","Significantly cheaper than adding the equivalent aftermarket"],"cons":["$7,500 is real money on top of an already expensive truck","Fuel economy drops 1\u20132 MPG versus stock 315/70","Highway noise increases with 37s","Resale value benefit is partial \u2014 used 37 Package trucks command a premium, but not the full $7,500 option cost"]},{"id":"raptor-gen-buyers-snapshot","title":"Gen 1 vs Gen 2 vs Gen 3 Raptor: Which One to Buy","vehicle":"raptor","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"buyers_guide","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":35000,"high":95000},"tools_required":["inspection light","OBD2 scanner"],"skills_required":["used vehicle inspection"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2014","2017-2020","2021-present"],"tags":["buying","gen1","gen2","gen3","raptor-r","6.2","ecoboost","comparison"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Ford F-150 Raptor SVT specs \u2014 6.2L V8","url":"https://media.ford.com"},{"title":"2017 Raptor EcoBoost 3.5 EPA fuel economy","url":"https://www.fueleconomy.gov"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The honest comparison: Gen 1 SVT is the last naturally-aspirated Raptor, Gen 2 is the performance sweet spot, Gen 3 is overpriced unless you need the R.","description":"The Raptor has three distinct generations with meaningful differences under the skin \u2014 not styling updates. Picking the wrong one based on price alone is a common mistake on the used market.\n\nThe original. Ford Performance's SVT division built this one, and it shows. The 6.2L V8 is a naturally-aspirated engine producing 411 hp and 434 lb-ft of torque, and it has aged well. No turbos means no intercooler heat soak, no cam phasers to worry about, and a more straightforward maintenance profile than the EcoBoost that replaced it.\n\nThe suspension is Fox Racing Shox internal-bypass units \u2014 not the Gen 2/3 Live Valve electronics, but still capable hardware. Front travel is 11.2 inches, rear 12 inches. These trucks can genuinely fly across rough terrain.\n\nThe honest drawback: fuel economy is brutal. Expect 11\u201314 MPG in mixed driving. Towing payload is solid, but the 6.2 gets outrun by the 3.5 EcoBoost in most performance metrics. In 2026, a clean low-mileage Gen 1 SVT costs $28,000\u2013$45,000 depending on mileage and condition. That's a lot of money for a truck with a 90,000-mile engine and no factory warranty.\n\n**Who it's for:** Someone who wants fewer electronics over electronics, prefers naturally-aspirated, and has a realistic budget for fuel and eventual engine work.\n\nFord skipped 2015\u20132016 for a complete redesign. The Gen 2 is larger, more sophisticated, and significantly faster than the Gen 1. The 3.5L twin-turbo EcoBoost produces 450 hp and 510 lb-ft of torque \u2014 more than the V8 in nearly every metric that matters for desert running.\n\nFox Live Valve shocks arrived with Gen 2. The system reads terrain inputs multiple times per second and adjusts damping continuously. It is genuinely impressive technology, and it works. The drawback: Live Valve shocks need rebuilds at roughly 20,000\u201330,000 miles of hard use. A full set of four rebuilds from a qualified shop runs $1,200\u2013$2,000. That's real money, but the alternative \u2014 running worn Live Valve shocks \u2014 defeats the entire point of the truck.\n\nGen 2 also introduced the Terrain Management System with Baja mode. On flat desert, with Baja mode engaged, the Gen 2 is faster and more planted than almost anything in its class.\n\nThe cam phaser issue is Gen 2's main reliability red flag. The 3.5 EcoBoost had documented cam phaser rattle on cold starts, especially in early production (2017\u20132018). Ford issued TSBs and made running changes. Inspect any used Gen 2 carefully for cam phaser noise \u2014 a cold-start rattle that disappears at temperature is the tell. If it's advanced, engine teardown is coming.\n\n**Used pricing in 2026:** $42,000\u2013$62,000 for clean examples.\n\n**Who it's for:** The performance buyer who wants the best balance of speed, suspension capability, and relative reliability. This is the sweet spot.\n\nGen 3 brought a 37\" tire option from factory (the 37 Package), upgraded brakes, 4.10 gears on the 37 Package, and improved Fox Live Valve shocks. The base engine is still the 3.5 EcoBoost, now rated at 450 hp (same as Gen 2 on paper, with calibration updates).\n\nThe headline addition is the **Raptor R (2023+)**: a 5.2L supercharged V8 Predator engine from the Mustang Shelby GT500, detuned to 700 hp for the truck application. The Raptor R is genuinely extraordinary \u2014 and genuinely expensive. MSRP was around $109,000 new. Used examples trade at $90,000\u2013$120,000+ depending on mileage and options.\n\nThe base Gen 3 is harder to justify. It's more capable than the Gen 2 in measured terms but the price delta on the used market is real. A Gen 3 base Raptor costs $58,000\u2013$80,000 used \u2014 meaningfully more than an equivalent Gen 2 \u2014 for improvements that most buyers won't access on their actual use.\n\n**Who it's for:** The 37 Package buyer who wants factory 37s and bigger brakes, or the Raptor R buyer who wants the most capable truck available and has the budget.\n\nGen 2 is the sweet spot for most buyers. It offers the biggest jump in capability from Gen 1, is available in volume on the used market, and the EcoBoost + Live Valve combination is proven at this point. Inspect cold-start cam phaser behavior carefully and budget for a shock rebuild if the truck has high hard-use miles.\n\nGen 1 SVT is a valid buy if you want fewer electronics and a naturally-aspirated engine with no electronics anxiety. The fuel economy is a real penalty.\n\nGen 3 base is hard to recommend at current used prices unless you specifically need the 37 Package hardware. The Raptor R is in its own category entirely.","steps":["Determine your budget and intended use before looking at specific trucks","On Gen 2 inspection: cold-start the engine and listen for cam phaser rattle before it warms up","On Gen 2/3 inspection: check the Fox Live Valve shock condition \u2014 look for oil on the shock bodies","Verify the 37 Package on Gen 3 via window sticker if the seller claims it","Pull a full vehicle history report and check service records for shock work","Test all Terrain Management modes and Baja mode during the test drive"],"pros":["Gen 2 is the performance and value sweet spot on the used market","All generations share strong aftermarket support","Fox shocks are rebuildable \u2014 high-mileage trucks are not necessarily worn-out trucks"],"cons":["Gen 1 fuel economy is a genuine operating cost issue","Gen 2 cam phaser TSB is a real risk on uninspected trucks","Gen 3 base pricing is hard to justify versus Gen 2 on the used market","Live Valve shock rebuilds are not cheap and require specialized shops"]},{"id":"raptor-vs-trx","title":"Raptor vs. Ram TRX: Which One for What","vehicle":"raptor","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"comparison","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":65000,"high":120000},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-present"],"tags":["trx","comparison","raptor-r","v8","ecoboost","performance","towing"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Ram 1500 TRX specs and pricing","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/ram_1500_trx.html"},{"title":"Ford F-150 Raptor gen 3 specs","url":"https://media.ford.com"},{"title":"TRX vs Raptor fuel economy comparison","url":"https://www.fueleconomy.gov"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The TRX is faster, louder, and more theatrical. The Raptor is more usable, better on fuel, and has a deeper aftermarket. Both are excellent trucks \u2014 the right one depends on what you actually do with it.","description":"The Ram TRX and Ford F-150 Raptor occupy the same market position \u2014 high-performance factory desert trucks \u2014 and are direct competitors. The comparison invites fanboy arguments. This is not that. Both trucks are legitimately impressive. The differences are real and matter for different use cases.\n\n**Ram TRX:** 6.2L supercharged HEMI V8, 702 hp, 650 lb-ft torque. 8-speed automatic. This engine is related to the Hellcat platform. It is genuinely violent in a way that the EcoBoost cannot match for raw character. 0\u201360 mph in approximately 4.5 seconds.\n\n**Ford Raptor (Gen 3 base):** 3.5L twin-turbo EcoBoost V6, 450 hp, 510 lb-ft torque. 10-speed automatic. 0\u201360 mph in approximately 5.4 seconds. This is fast. It is not TRX fast.\n\n**Ford Raptor R:** 5.2L supercharged Predator V8, 700 hp, 640 lb-ft torque. Same architecture as the GT500 engine, detuned slightly for the truck application. The performance gap between the Raptor R and TRX is small. The Raptor R is the right comparison to the TRX from a powertrain perspective.\n\nBoth trucks run Fox shocks with similar long-travel architecture. The TRX has 13.1\" front travel, 13.0\" rear. The Raptor Gen 3 (non-37 Package) has 13.0\" front, 14.1\" rear. These are close enough that real-world capability over whoops and rough terrain is similar \u2014 the driver and terrain matter more than the fractional travel advantage.\n\nBoth trucks have been run competitively in desert race conditions. Both are demonstrably capable far beyond what their drivers typically ask of them.\n\n**TRX EPA rated:** 10 city / 14 highway. Real-world mixed driving: 11\u201313 MPG.\n\n**Raptor Gen 3 EPA rated:** 15 city / 18 highway. Real-world mixed driving: 14\u201317 MPG.\n\nThe fuel economy gap is real and sustained. At 15,000 miles per year, the TRX costs approximately $1,500\u2013$2,000 more per year in fuel at current prices. Over five years, that's $7,500\u2013$10,000 in additional fuel cost compared to the base Raptor. The Raptor R is better than the TRX but worse than the base Raptor (EPA rates the Raptor R at 10 city / 16 highway).\n\n**TRX:** Max tow rating 8,100 lbs.\n\n**Raptor (Gen 3 base):** Max tow rating 8,200 lbs.\n\nBoth are limited by the high-performance suspension tuning \u2014 the suspension that makes these trucks exceptional in desert terrain is not optimized for heavy towing. If towing over 6,000 lbs regularly is part of the use case, neither truck is the ideal choice for towing-as-primary-purpose. Both can tow, and either handles 6,000 lbs without drama. But a standard F-150 Lariat or Ram Limited will tow more stably and comfortably.\n\nThe Raptor has a significantly larger and more developed aftermarket. It has been on the market since 2010 in one form or another, and the community around it is enormous. Suspension kits, bumpers, long-travel upper control arms, skid plates, lighting \u2014 all exist in volume with proven options.\n\nThe TRX launched in 2021. Its aftermarket is growing but thinner than the Raptor's. If you plan to modify the truck significantly, the Raptor's ecosystem is more manageable to navigate.\n\nBuy the TRX if: you want the V8 experience, don't tow regularly, can absorb the fuel cost, and the 200 additional hp matters to your use case.\n\nBuy the Raptor if: you want a more efficient daily driver that is still capable on desert terrain, plan to modify the truck with a developed aftermarket, or can't justify the TRX fuel bill.\n\nBuy the Raptor R if: you want V8 character and sound, will accept the price premium, and are comparing it directly to the TRX on performance terms.\n\nNeither truck is a mistake. They're both genuinely overbuilt in the best way. The mistake is buying either one and then primarily driving it to work on paved roads \u2014 at which point the fuel bill is the story.\n\n- Better fuel economy ($1,500\u2013$2,000/yr savings vs TRX)\n- Deeper, more developed aftermarket\n- 10-speed transmission is smooth and well-calibrated\n\n- The V8 character is a different character \u2014 the EcoBoost doesn't replicate it\n- TRX is faster by a meaningful margin (Raptor base vs TRX)\n- 702 hp vs 450 hp is not a close comparison in raw performance terms","steps":["Determine your actual use \u2014 daily driving vs. trail vs. desert vs. towing","Model the annual fuel cost difference using your actual miles driven","Test drive both trucks if possible \u2014 the powertrain character difference is visceral and personal","Evaluate aftermarket support if you plan modifications","Check current used pricing \u2014 both trucks trade at significant premiums over a few years ago"]},{"id":"ecoboost-3-5-cam-phaser","title":"Gen 2 Raptor 3.5L EcoBoost Cam Phaser Rattle \u2014 What It Is and What to Ask a Seller","vehicle":"raptor","category":"engine","subcategory":"timing","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":4500},"tools_required":["nothing for inspection \u2014 a cold engine and your ears"],"skills_required":["used vehicle inspection","cold-start listening"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2017-2020"],"tags":["ecoboost","cam-phaser","3.5","engine","tsb","reliability","buying"],"parts":[{"name":"Cam phaser replacement (dealer repair)","vendor":"Ford Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":3500}],"citations":[{"title":"Ford TSB 19-2345 \u2014 3.5L EcoBoost cam phaser","url":"https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2019/MC-10163804-9999.pdf"},{"title":"NHTSA complaints \u2014 2017-2019 F-150 Raptor timing","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov/vehicle/2017/FORD/F-150/PU/4WD"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 3.5L EcoBoost cam phaser rattle is a real issue on early Gen 2 Raptors (2017\u20132018). On a cold start, a grinding or rattling noise from the top of the engine that disappears within 30 seconds is the tell. If a seller won't let you cold-start the truck, walk away.","description":"The variable cam timing phasers on the 3.5L EcoBoost work by varying oil pressure to advance or retard camshaft timing. The design is sound, but early production Gen 2 Raptors (and F-150s with the same engine) had phaser wear issues \u2014 specifically, the phasers developed slop that caused a mechanical rattle on cold starts before oil pressure built fully.\n\nIt is a grinding or clattering sound from the top of the engine, most prominent in the first 10\u201330 seconds of a cold start. It may sound like a timing chain rattle, a loose heat shield, or a loud valve tick. It disappears as the engine reaches operating temperature \u2014 or at least diminishes significantly \u2014 because warm oil builds pressure faster and controls the phasers more effectively.\n\nThis is not a subtle noise. If the phasers are failing or failed, you will hear it. The confusion comes from a truck that has been sitting overnight and shows a very mild tick that disappears almost immediately \u2014 that can be normal oil drainback, not a phaser issue. The phaser rattle is louder and persists longer.\n\nFord acknowledged the issue through Technical Service Bulletins. TSB 19-2345 (and related bulletins) addressed the 3.5L EcoBoost phaser concern, covering revised phasers and updated engine calibration to manage oil delivery at startup. Revised phasers (stronger, revised internal valving) were phased into production across 2018\u20132020 model years.\n\n**What the TSB did and didn't cover:**\n- Trucks with documented cam phaser noise before the TSB: Ford covered repair under warranty if within mileage and time limits\n- Out-of-warranty repairs: not covered \u2014 the cam phaser replacement costs $2,500\u2013$4,500 at a dealer depending on which phasers fail and how much timing chain work is involved\n- The revised phasers from the TSB are now the standard replacement part \u2014 if you're replacing phasers, you get the updated design\n\nIgnoring advanced cam phaser wear can lead to timing chain issues. The phasers interact with the timing chain tensioners \u2014 a phaser with significant slop creates shock loading on the chain system. This doesn't happen quickly or in one event, but continued operation with documented phaser wear accelerates timing chain wear.\n\nAn engine with a worn cam phaser that also has a stretched timing chain is a $6,000\u2013$10,000 problem. This is not the typical outcome, but it's the outcome if someone buys a truck with the noise, ignores it, and runs it hard.\n\n1. **Does it rattle on cold start?** Direct question. If they say no, verify it yourself.\n2. **Can you cold-start the truck for me?** If they refuse or say the truck is already warmed up and can't be cooled down, that's a signal.\n3. **Has any cam phaser work been done?** Ask for documentation. A phaser repair is a meaningful service visit \u2014 it should be in the service history.\n4. **What year was it built?** 2017 and early 2018 production trucks are the highest-risk group.\n\nCold-start the engine. Stand near the driver's side of the engine bay. Listen for 45\u201360 seconds. If there's a rattle that sounds mechanical and comes from the top of the engine, you've found your answer. If it's clean and quiet from startup, you can proceed with more confidence.\n\nA mechanic's inspection can go further \u2014 they can pull an OBD2 history and look for timing-related codes that may have been cleared before the sale.","steps":["Arrange to inspect the truck cold \u2014 not warmed up","Open the hood before starting","Start the engine and stand next to the engine bay, driver's side","Listen for 45\u201360 seconds for any grinding, clattering, or ticking from the top of the engine","If noise is present: ask for service history and walk away if none exists","If noise is absent: verify with an OBD2 scan for any cleared timing-related codes","Factor any needed repair into your offer \u2014 dealer cam phaser work is $2,500\u2013$4,500"],"pros":["Early detection is low-cost \u2014 a cold start and your ears","Revised phasers from Ford's TSB are better than the originals","2019+ production trucks benefited from running changes and are lower risk"],"cons":["Early 2017\u20132018 trucks require active inspection \u2014 do not assume it's fine","Out-of-warranty repair is expensive ($2,500\u2013$4,500)","Advanced phaser wear can affect the timing chain, multiplying repair cost"]},{"id":"desert-running-coolant-and-oil","title":"What Hard Desert and Whoop Running Does to an EcoBoost \u2014 Oil, Cooling, and Maintenance Intervals","vehicle":"raptor","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"fluid_intervals","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":2.0},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":200},"tools_required":["oil drain pan","funnel","oil filter wrench","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["oil change","fluid inspection","temperature monitoring"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2017-2020","2021-present"],"tags":["maintenance","oil","coolant","heat","ecoboost","intercooler","desert","intervals"],"parts":[{"name":"Motorcraft 5W-30 Full Synthetic Oil (6 quarts)","vendor":"Ford Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Motorcraft FL-500S oil filter","vendor":"Ford Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Motorcraft VC-7-B Gold Coolant concentrate","vendor":"Ford Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":25}],"citations":[{"title":"Ford F-150 Raptor owner's manual \u2014 maintenance schedule","url":"https://www.ford.com/support/how-tos/owner-manuals/"},{"title":"EcoBoost heat soak and intercooler technical overview","url":"https://www.ecoboostforum.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Hard desert running generates thermal loads the Raptor's standard maintenance schedule wasn't built around. Oil temps spike in whoops, the intercooler heat-soaks after extended runs, and coolant condition degrades faster at continuous high load. Adjust intervals to match how you actually use the truck.","description":"The Ford Intelligent Oil-Life Monitor (IOLM) calculates oil change intervals based on engine load, RPM, and temperature. In highway use it's accurate. In hard desert running \u2014 Baja-style whoops at speed, sustained off-road throttle, extended Baja mode operation \u2014 the thermal and mechanical load on the oil is higher than the IOLM's typical model assumes. The result is that the indicator may read \"30% remaining\" when the oil has been through conditions that justify a change.\n\nHigh-speed whoop running involves continuous, rapid suspension cycling. Every compression event pushes the suspension at or near full compression speed. The shocks manage most of this, but the engine and drivetrain are also in continuous high-load operation: the truck is accelerating repeatedly over each crest, the engine is operating at high throttle, and the turbochargers are running hot.\n\nOil temps in hard Raptor use can reach 230\u2013260\u00b0F sustained. Modern full synthetic oil handles this well \u2014 but sustained operation at the high end of that range oxidizes oil faster than highway driving. The base oil life extends shorter when the thermal exposure is consistent.\n\n**Practical guidance for hard desert use:**\n- Hard desert day (4+ hours of sustained off-highway running): change oil within 1,000\u20131,500 miles of that use, regardless of the IOLM reading\n- Regular trail use (weekly, moderate intensity): drop to 5,000-mile oil intervals rather than the OEM extended interval\n- Light trail use with mostly highway miles: OEM intervals are fine\n\nUse Motorcraft full synthetic 5W-30. The 3.5L EcoBoost was engineered around this viscosity. Going heavier does not improve high-temp protection meaningfully and can increase fuel consumption.\n\nThe twin-turbo 3.5L EcoBoost compresses intake air, and compressed air is hot air. The charge air cooler (intercooler) removes heat from the compressed charge before it enters the engine. This works well in continuous-motion situations where airflow over the intercooler is consistent.\n\nIn stop-and-go situations or extended idle after hard running, airflow through the intercooler drops dramatically. The accumulated heat from the turbochargers soaks into the intake system. Charge air temperature rises, and power output drops noticeably \u2014 sometimes significantly. This is heat soak, and it's inherent to the EcoBoost design.\n\n**Managing heat soak:**\n- After a hard run in Baja mode, idle for 2\u20133 minutes before shutting down \u2014 this lets the turbos cool with oil flowing before the engine stops\n- Avoid extended idle in the sun after a hard run \u2014 if you're parked and cooling down, the engine off is better than extended idle without airflow\n- Aftermarket intercooler upgrades (larger core, higher-efficiency bar-and-plate cores from firms like Mishimoto or Whipple) reduce heat soak recovery time\n\nThe 3.5L EcoBoost uses Motorcraft Gold (OAT) coolant. Do not mix with green coolant or Dex-Cool. The Ford owner's manual recommends coolant service at 100,000 miles or 6 years \u2014 in hard desert use, drop this to 60,000\u201375,000 miles.\n\nCheck coolant concentration seasonally (50/50 mix for most climates, 70/30 mix for extreme cold). More importantly, check color and condition. OAT coolant should be orange-amber and translucent. Murky, brown, or opaque coolant has degraded and should be replaced regardless of mileage.\n\nThe 10R80 automatic transmission is a sealed unit with no conventional dipstick. Ford specifies Motorcraft MERCON ULP fluid. The OEM change interval for normal use is \"lifetime.\" For hard off-road use \u2014 particularly extended Baja mode operation where the transmission is managing high heat \u2014 many Raptor owners change transmission fluid at 60,000\u201380,000 miles. The cost of the fluid is trivial compared to a transmission rebuild.\n\nFront and rear differentials use 75W-140 full synthetic gear oil. The transfer case uses Motorcraft MERCON LV ATF. Both should be changed at 60,000-mile intervals in normal use, and more frequently if you've run through water deep enough to reach the differential vents. Water intrusion in differential fluid turns it milky \u2014 inspect immediately after any significant water crossing.","steps":["After any hard desert day: inspect oil color on the dipstick \u2014 dark brown or black is a change indicator","Track miles since last oil change separately from the IOLM reading if you do hard trail use","Change oil within 1,000\u20131,500 miles following any extended hard desert day","Check coolant level and color before any major trip","After extended hard running: idle 2\u20133 minutes before shutdown to cool turbos","Inspect differential fluid at each oil change \u2014 look for milky discoloration","Budget a transmission fluid change at 60,000\u201380,000 miles if using the truck hard"],"pros":["Full synthetic oil handles Raptor operating temps well when intervals are adjusted appropriately","The 3.5 EcoBoost responds well to proactive maintenance \u2014 neglected examples have more issues","Intercooler upgrades reduce heat soak if it becomes a consistent issue"],"cons":["The IOLM doesn't account for sustained hard off-road use \u2014 don't rely on it exclusively","Heat soak is inherent to the EcoBoost design \u2014 it cannot be entirely eliminated","Transmission fluid access requires a dealer or a shop with the correct fluid and fill procedure"]},{"id":"raptor-recovery-points","title":"Factory Raptor Tow Hooks vs. Proper Recovery Points","vehicle":"raptor","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery_points","type":"how_to","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":3.0},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":600},"tools_required":["torque wrench","impact wrench","metric sockets","thread locker"],"skills_required":["recovery equipment knowledge","basic wrenching","torque spec adherence"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2014","2017-2020","2021-present"],"tags":["recovery","recovery-points","tow-hooks","d-ring","kinetic","snatch-strap","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn Recovery Shackles 3/4\" (pair)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":85},{"name":"Factor 55 FlatLink shackle receiver (pair)","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Addicted Offroad front recovery points \u2014 Raptor-specific","vendor":"Addicted Offroad","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Kinetic recovery rope 30' 3/4\"","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":150}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB kinetic rope safety and rated recovery points","url":"https://www.arb.com.au/recovery"},{"title":"Warn recovery equipment ratings","url":"https://www.warn.com/recovery-gear"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The factory Raptor tow hooks are painted-on frame mounts rated for towing assistance \u2014 they are NOT rated for kinetic recovery. A kinetic strap snatch on an unrated hook can pull the hook free of the frame with enough force to send it through a windshield. Use rated recovery points.","description":"This is not a gray area. Tow hooks and recovery points are different products with different ratings for different forces. The distinction matters because kinetic recovery (snatch straps, kinetic ropes) generates peak forces of 2\u20134x the static pull weight \u2014 the elasticity of the strap stores energy that releases as a shock load. A 20,000 lb rated tow hook sees forces it was not designed for in a kinetic recovery event.\n\nThe Raptor's painted red tow hooks are frame-mounted steel hooks. They are adequate for:\n- Connecting a tow strap to an assisting vehicle for a gentle, low-speed pull out of a mild situation\n- Winching at low, controlled speeds where peak force stays within the hook's rating\n- Connecting a standard tow line to a tow truck\n\nThey are not adequate for:\n- Kinetic recovery with a snatch strap or kinetic rope\n- High-speed pull-outs on any terrain\n- Any recovery situation where vehicle weight and momentum are significant\n\nFord does not publish a rated load limit for the factory tow hooks as recovery points because they are not rated as recovery points. That tells you what you need to know.\n\nA rated recovery point is engineered, tested, and stamped with a working load limit (WLL) and a minimum breaking strength (MBS). The WLL for a front recovery point on a vehicle of the Raptor's weight (approximately 5,400 lbs) should be rated above vehicle weight \u2014 8,000\u201312,000 lb WLL is a reasonable minimum.\n\nMounting matters as much as rating. A recovery point bolted through the bumper's outer skin is not a recovery point. Recovery points must mount to structural frame members directly, with sufficient hardware to distribute load into the frame without pulling through.\n\n**Factory tow hook shackle upgrade:** Factor 55 and a few others make D-ring or shackle receivers that replace or attach to the factory tow hook mounting. These use the factory hook mount but add a rated shackle attach point. Verify the specific product is rated as a recovery point, not a tow accessory.\n\n**Aftermarket bumper with integrated recovery points:** An aftermarket steel front bumper with proper rated recovery points built in is the cleanest solution. Warn, Addicted Offroad, and ARB all make Raptor-specific options with engineer-rated front shackle mounts. This route also gives you a proper winch platform if you plan to add a winch.\n\n**Rear recovery:** The factory rear tow hitch receiver is a valid recovery attachment point when used with a proper hitch-mount shackle receiver. A quality receiver-mount like a Factor 55 FlatLink gives you a rated recovery attach at the rear without modifying the truck.\n\nIf you're running a kinetic recovery rope (preferred for all self-recovery and most assisted recovery), the shock loads on attachment points are significant. The kinetic rope stretches 20\u201330% under load and releases that energy rapidly. On a 5,400 lb truck stuck in soft sand at moderate angles, peak recovery forces can reach 12,000\u201320,000 lb in the stretch-release phase.\n\nAttach those forces to a painted tow hook and you have a hazard. Attach to a properly rated recovery point mounted to frame, and the physics work the way they're supposed to.\n\n**Use rated points every time. No exceptions.**","steps":["Assess the factory front tow hooks \u2014 understand what they are and what they are not","Decide on your approach: shackle receiver upgrade, aftermarket bumper, or combination","If using a shackle receiver: verify the receiver's working load limit and confirm it mounts to the factory hook frame attachment","If installing an aftermarket bumper: follow the manufacturer's torque specs exactly \u2014 these are structural fasteners","Torque all mounting hardware to specification and apply thread locker on non-prevailing-torque fasteners","Mark your rated recovery points so any person helping you in an emergency knows exactly where to attach","Never attach a kinetic strap or snatch strap to an unrated hook, bumper tab, or frame-only attachment that bypasses rated hardware"],"pros":["Aftermarket bumpers with rated recovery points are a complete solution: recovery + winch platform + real protection","Rated recovery points cost far less than the alternative outcomes","Factor 55 and similar shackle receivers are a budget-accessible upgrade that uses factory mounting"],"cons":["Aftermarket bumpers add weight and cost ($600\u2013$2,500 depending on brand)","Factory tow hooks look fine \u2014 which makes it straightforward to convince yourself they're adequate when they are not","Recovery point installation on some bumpers requires removing the factory bumper entirely (not a difficult job, but a full afternoon)"]},{"id":"raptor-winch","title":"Winch for the Gen 1 Raptor \u2014 12,000 lb Minimum for a 6,000 lb Truck","vehicle":"raptor","category":"recovery","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":499,"high":1299},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","wire strippers","crimping tool","multimeter"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wiring basics"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2014"],"tags":["winch","recovery","electrical","synthetic-rope"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn M12000-S Spydura Synthetic Rope Winch (12,000 lb)","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Warn Zeon 12-S Platinum Synthetic Rope Winch (12,000 lb)","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"Smittybilt X20 12K Wireless Synthetic Rope Winch (12,000 lb)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"Warn VR EVO 12-S Synthetic Rope Winch (12,000 lb)","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":549}],"citations":[{"title":"Winch capacity selection guide \u2014 Warn Industries technical FAQ","url":"https://www.warn.com/offroad/winch-selection-guide"},{"title":"Synthetic vs wire rope for winches \u2014 Expedition Portal community guide","url":"https://expeditionportal.com/forum/threads/synthetic-vs-wire-rope-winch.html"},{"title":"Gen 1 Raptor winch electrical requirements \u2014 RaptorForum","url":"https://www.raptorforum.com/threads/winch-electrical-requirements-gen1.html"}],"alternatives":["raptor-front-bumper"],"summary":"At 5,500\u20136,000 lbs of curb weight, the Gen 1 Raptor needs a 12,000 lb winch minimum \u2014 and it needs an aftermarket front bumper with a winch cradle before any of this applies, because the stock plastic fascia has nowhere to mount one.","description":"The standard winch sizing rule is 1.5x the vehicle's gross weight. For a Gen 1 Raptor at ~5,500 lbs curb (add fuel, gear, and passengers to approach 6,500 lbs loaded), that puts the minimum at roughly 9,750 lbs \u2014 but in practice, you buy 12,000 lbs. The reason is that winch ratings are measured with a single layer of rope on the drum; every additional layer reduces pulling power by roughly 10% because the effective spool diameter increases. By the third or fourth layer \u2014 which is where you are when you're pulled deep into an obstacle \u2014 a 9,500 lb winch can be delivering 7,000 lbs of pull. A 12,000 lb winch in the same scenario is still above your weight. Buying down is the wrong call on a truck this heavy.\n\nThe aftermarket bumper prerequisite is genuine: the Gen 1 Raptor's factory front fascia is a plastic-and-foam assembly with no structural winch mount provision. It cannot accept a fairlead. It cannot carry the load transfer of a winch pull. You need to pair this purchase with the raptor-front-bumper entry \u2014 specifically a bumper that lists its winch cradle as rated for the winch weight you're running (most quality Raptor bumpers specify 12,000 lb cradle compatibility).\n\nSynthetic rope is the right choice over steel wire on a truck driven at high speed. Wire rope stores enormous energy under tension and can snap back with lethal force if it parts; synthetic rope goes limp when it fails. Synthetic also doesn't develop the metal burrs that wire develops over time, making it safer to handle. The tradeoff is cost (~$100\u2013$150 more) and UV sensitivity \u2014 synthetic rope should be protected from extended direct sun when not in use and inspected annually. Both Warn and Smittybilt now ship many 12K units with synthetic as standard. The Warn M12000-S and VR EVO 12-S represent reliable mid-tier and budget-tier options respectively. The Zeon 12-S is Warn's premium line \u2014 faster line speed, aircraft-grade aluminum drum, and a lighter profile.\n\nThe electrical side matters on a 12K winch. A full-load pull on a 12,000 lb winch draws 400\u2013500 amps. The Gen 1 Raptor's stock electrical system can handle short pulls, but sustained winching \u2014 particularly with the engine at idle \u2014 can drain the battery faster than the alternator recovers it. Running the engine at 1,500\u20132,000 RPM during a winch pull significantly improves alternator output. A second battery in the bed (connected via an isolator) is the proper solution if you're using the winch regularly on desert expeditions.","steps":["Install the aftermarket front bumper first (raptor-front-bumper). Confirm the winch cradle is rated for 12,000 lb use.","Mount the winch in the cradle per the bumper manufacturer's instructions. Most use four 1/2\" grade-8 bolts. Torque to spec.","Thread the rope through the fairlead and spool it evenly onto the drum. Maintain 10\u201312 pounds of tension on the rope as you spool \u2014 loose first layers cause spool tangles under load.","Run the power leads (positive and negative, minimum 2-gauge cable) from the winch to the battery. Install a circuit breaker (500A, mounted near the battery) on the positive lead \u2014 this is not optional.","Connect the control lead to the solenoid and route the remote control cable into the cab.","Test: with the truck running at 1,500 RPM, make a short pull against a rated anchor. Confirm solenoid engagement, verify fairlead alignment, and check all connections for heat after a 30-second pull."],"pros":["12,000 lb rating provides real-world adequate pull on a loaded Raptor in soft terrain","Synthetic rope is safer than wire under tension failure","Warn's warranty and service network is the industry benchmark","Wireless remote option (Smittybilt X20) eliminates the cable that gets tangled and pinched"],"cons":["Requires an aftermarket front bumper \u2014 you cannot install this on the stock plastic fascia","Sustained winching at low engine RPM can drain the battery; run the engine during pulls","12,000 lb winches are heavy (85\u2013105 lbs) and add to the front end weight","Budget units (Smittybilt X20) have inconsistent long-term reliability compared to Warn; fine for occasional use, less suitable for frequent desert recovery work"]},{"id":"fox-shock-rebuild-schedule","title":"Fox Live Valve and Internal-Bypass Shock Rebuild Intervals on the Gen 2/3 Raptor","vehicle":"raptor","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":1200,"high":2200},"tools_required":["OBD2 scanner","shock spanner wrench","inspection light"],"skills_required":["suspension inspection","reading shock condition"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2017-2020","2021-present"],"tags":["fox","live-valve","shocks","suspension","maintenance","rebuild"],"parts":[{"name":"Fox Live Valve shock rebuild (set of 4)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox authorized shop","price_estimate_usd":1600},{"name":"Fox 3.0 internal bypass shock rebuild (set of 4)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox authorized shop","price_estimate_usd":1400}],"citations":[{"title":"Fox Racing Shox service and rebuild","url":"https://www.ridefox.com/fox17/help.php?m=bike&id=service"},{"title":"Fox shock rebuild intervals \u2014 offroad use","url":"https://www.svtperformance.com/threads/fox-shock-rebuild-thread.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Gen 2 and Gen 3 Raptor Fox shocks need rebuilds at roughly 20,000\u201330,000 miles of hard desert or trail use. Worn shocks on a Raptor are a safety and handling issue, not a comfort complaint.","description":"The factory Fox Racing Shox on the Gen 2 and Gen 3 Raptor are not sealed lifetime units. They are rebuildable, and they need to be. The Live Valve electronics on Gen 2/3 can extend the range of a slightly worn shock, which paradoxically makes it more manageable to miss the point at which the shock body is overdue for service.\n\nThe Fox shocks on these trucks are high-performance internal-bypass units. \"Internal bypass\" means the shock has multiple stages of compression damping that progressively stiffen as the shock compresses further \u2014 this is how the Raptor can feel plush at low speeds and firm up dramatically when the terrain gets aggressive. That staged behavior depends on clean fluid and seals that hold pressure.\n\nDesert running degrades shocks differently than highway driving. Repeated high-speed whoops, wash-outs, and rocky terrain heat and aerate the oil more aggressively than any combination of road miles. A truck with 40,000 highway miles may have shocks in better condition than a truck with 15,000 miles of hard Sonoran Desert running.\n\n**Useful interval guidance:**\n- Casual trail use (mild dirt roads, occasional off-highway): rebuild at 30,000\u201340,000 miles\n- Active trail use (regular off-highway, some desert): rebuild at 20,000\u201330,000 miles\n- Hard desert use (whoops, high-speed off-road, Baja-style): rebuild at 10,000\u201320,000 miles\n\nPhysical inspection tells you more than mileage. Look for:\n\n- **Oil weeping on the shock body** \u2014 any visible oil tracking down the shock tube means a seal is failing. This is the clearest indicator.\n- **Soft or vague feel over small bumps** \u2014 internal bypass shocks that are worn out lose their low-speed damping first. The ride gets floaty.\n- **Bottoming on impacts it should handle** \u2014 if the truck is topping or bottoming on terrain it handled comfortably before, the shock is past due.\n- **Live Valve fault codes** \u2014 a failing Live Valve actuator will throw codes. The electronics can fail independently from the shock body, but they're often related.\n\nNot every shop can work on Fox Live Valve shocks. The Live Valve system requires electronic calibration after any work on the actuator. Look specifically for:\n\n- **Fox authorized rebuild centers** \u2014 Fox maintains a network of authorized service shops. Find one through Fox's dealer locator. These shops have the proprietary calibration tools.\n- **King and Sway-A-Way** \u2014 some off-road performance shops that build King or custom bypass shocks can evaluate and rebuild Fox units, but verify they have the Live Valve calibration capability before committing.\n- **Local 4x4 shops without Fox authorization** \u2014 they can rebuild the shock body mechanically but cannot recalibrate the Live Valve electronics. This is a partial rebuild at best.\n\nExpect to pay $1,200\u2013$2,200 for a full four-shock rebuild including new seals, fresh fluid, and Live Valve recalibration. That's real money. It's also substantially cheaper than replacing all four shocks with new units, which runs $3,500\u2013$5,000 for the complete OEM Fox set.\n\nRunning worn Live Valve shocks doesn't strand you immediately. The truck continues to function. But worn shocks on a Raptor are a meaningful safety issue in hard use \u2014 a truck that's supposed to be stable at 80 mph across desert terrain becomes unpredictable when the suspension can't control body motion correctly. The handling envelope that makes these trucks safe at speed depends on the shocks working as designed.","steps":["Inspect shock bodies visually every 10,000 miles \u2014 look for oil tracking on all four shock tubes","Note any change in ride quality \u2014 softness over small bumps is an early indicator","Check for Live Valve fault codes with an OBD2 scanner","Find a Fox authorized rebuild center before the shocks fail, not after","Have all four shocks rebuilt as a set \u2014 staggered rebuild timing causes handling imbalance","After rebuild, re-torque all suspension fasteners to spec"],"pros":["Fox shocks are rebuildable \u2014 high-mileage trucks can be brought back to spec","Rebuilds cost significantly less than full replacement","Properly rebuilt shocks restore the full capability the truck was built around"],"cons":["Live Valve calibration requires authorized Fox shops \u2014 not every market has one","Cost is real ($1,200\u2013$2,200 for a full set rebuild)","Many used Raptors on the market have been hard-run with no shock service history"]},{"id":"rear-leaf-spring-pack-fade","title":"Why Gen 2 Raptor Rear Leaf Springs Sag and What to Do About It","vehicle":"raptor","category":"suspension","subcategory":"leaf_springs","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3.0,"high":6.0},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":2800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","spring compressors","torque wrench","metric sockets","breaker bar","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["leaf spring removal","torque spec adherence","suspension reassembly"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2017-2020"],"tags":["leaf-springs","rear-suspension","sag","gen2","springs","re-arch","add-a-leaf"],"parts":[{"name":"Deaver spring re-arch (Gen 2 Raptor rear pack)","vendor":"Deaver Springs","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Ford Racing rear leaf spring pack replacement (Gen 2)","vendor":"Ford Racing","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"Roadmaster Active Suspension leaf spring add-on","vendor":"Roadmaster","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"Rancho RS66R leaf spring add-a-leaf kit (F-150 compatible)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"Deaver Springs Raptor leaf spring service","url":"https://www.deaversprings.com"},{"title":"Gen 2 Raptor rear spring sag community documentation","url":"https://www.raptorforum.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Gen 2 Raptor rear leaf springs sag under hard use \u2014 particularly trucks that see regular desert running, heavy loads, or frequent high-speed whoops. Re-arching is the budget fix. A full spring swap is the right fix. An add-a-leaf is a Band-Aid.","description":"The Gen 2 Raptor's rear suspension is a multi-leaf spring pack, not the coilover setup many performance trucks use. Ford chose leaf springs for load-carrying capacity and to keep towing ratings viable. The tradeoff is that leaf springs take a set under sustained heavy load or extreme impact \u2014 they sag, and the truck sits lower in the rear than it did from the factory.\n\nThe factory rear leaf pack was designed for a range of uses from highway cruising to moderate desert running. Sustained high-speed whoop running \u2014 the kind of use the Raptor was built to do \u2014 generates repeated high-impact compression loads on the spring pack. Over time, the spring steel takes a permanent set and doesn't return to its original arc. The truck sits lower at the rear, ride quality changes, and handling balance shifts.\n\nThis is not a defect. It's wear. But it's worth understanding because a used Raptor with a sagged rear sits noticeably lower than a fresh truck, handles differently, and may bottom out more easily.\n\n**Signs of rear spring sag:**\n- Rear of truck sits lower than factory spec \u2014 measure from the ground to the top of the rear wheel arch and compare to factory spec\n- Rear bottoms out on impacts it previously handled\n- Truck looks tail-low, especially compared to photos from new\n\nA spring shop (Deaver, National Spring, or a local spring shop) removes the spring pack, heats the individual leaves, and re-bends them back to the factory arc. This restores original ride height and spring rate. It is a legitimate repair, not a hack.\n\n**Realistic expectations:**\n- Re-arched springs will sag again under the same use conditions \u2014 the spring steel has memory and was already fatigued once\n- Re-arching is appropriate for trucks that see moderate use and where budget is the primary constraint\n- Not appropriate for trucks that will continue to see hard desert use \u2014 the re-arched springs will sag faster than new springs\n\nReplacing the spring pack with new Ford Racing springs (or a quality aftermarket equivalent) restores original spring rate and arc with new steel. This is the correct long-term fix for trucks that see real use.\n\nFord Racing offers replacement leaf pack assemblies specific to the Gen 2 Raptor. Installation is a straightforward bolt-in job \u2014 remove the U-bolts, drop the axle, swap the pack, reinstall. A good shop can do this in 3\u20135 hours.\n\nAftermarket options include Deaver's Gen 2 Raptor-specific spring packs. Deaver builds springs for off-road race trucks and their Raptor-specific packs are respected. They offer options from near-stock to increased spring rate for more consistent bottoming control under heavy use.\n\nAn add-a-leaf adds a supplemental leaf to the existing pack, increasing spring rate and ride height. This is the cheapest option and the least appropriate.\n\nThe problem: adding a leaf to a fatigued pack increases spring rate but doesn't restore the arc of the original leaves \u2014 the sag comes from the main leaves losing their arc, and an add-a-leaf doesn't change that. The truck rides firmer but may still sit low. Add-a-leaf is appropriate for trucks that need increased load capacity or are routinely towing near their limit. It's not a spring sag fix.\n\nSeveral companies offer rear coilover conversion kits for the Gen 2 Raptor. These replace the leaf pack entirely with a coilover setup. This is a different truck after conversion \u2014 the ride quality changes significantly, load capacity drops, and the towing capability is reduced. This is a build decision, not a maintenance decision. It costs $3,500\u2013$6,000 in parts before installation.\n\nIf your use case is pure desert performance and you're not towing, it's worth investigating. If you use the truck for towing or carrying loads, this path eliminates that capability.","steps":["Measure current rear ride height \u2014 floor to wheel arch at the center of the rear wheel, truck empty, on flat ground","Compare to factory spec (varies slightly by trim \u2014 verify in Ford's documentation for your year)","If sag is 1\" or less: re-arch is appropriate if budget is constrained; spring pack replacement for long-term fix","If sag is more than 1.5\": full spring pack replacement is the correct call","Remove factory U-bolts and leaf spring mounting hardware (penetrating oil on hardware \u2014 these fasteners corrode)","Install new spring pack and new U-bolts \u2014 do not reuse old U-bolts on a spring pack replacement","Torque U-bolts and all leaf spring fasteners to factory spec (consult Ford service manual for your year)","Re-check torque after 100 miles"],"pros":["Full spring pack replacement restores factory performance at reasonable cost","Deaver and Ford Racing aftermarket options offer improved durability over OEM","Re-arch is a legitimate budget option for moderate-use trucks"],"cons":["Add-a-leaf does not fix spring sag \u2014 it changes spring rate only","Re-arched springs will sag again faster if use continues unchanged","Coilover conversion eliminates towing capacity and is a significant project","Leaf spring hardware (U-bolts, end bolts) corrodes \u2014 budget for replacement hardware regardless of which fix you choose"]},{"id":"raptor-shock-upgrade","title":"Shock Upgrade for the Gen 1 Raptor \u2014 Fox 3.0, King 2.5, and RCD Options","vehicle":"raptor","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":1800,"high":4500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","spring compressor","coilover spanner wrench","impact wrench"],"skills_required":["suspension work","coilover setup","alignment knowledge"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2014"],"tags":["suspension","shocks","coilover","fox","king","desert-runner"],"parts":[{"name":"Fox 3.0 Factory Series Front Coilovers (pair) \u2014 Raptor-specific valving","vendor":"Fox Factory","price_estimate_usd":999},{"name":"Fox 3.0 Factory Series Rear Shocks (pair) \u2014 Gen 1 Raptor","vendor":"Fox Factory","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"King 2.5 OEM Performance Front Coilovers (pair)","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"King 2.5 OEM Performance Rear Shocks (pair)","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"RCD Suspension 3.0 Front Coilovers (pair) \u2014 Raptor-specific","vendor":"RCD Suspension","price_estimate_usd":1299}],"citations":[{"title":"Fox 3.0 vs King 2.5 Raptor comparison \u2014 RaptorForum community","url":"https://www.raptorforum.com/threads/fox-3-0-vs-king-2-5-shock-comparison.html"},{"title":"Gen 1 Raptor suspension travel specs \u2014 Ford Media technical release","url":"https://media.ford.com/content/fordmedia/fna/us/en/news/2009/11/10/ford-svt-raptor.html"},{"title":"Bypass shock explained \u2014 Fox Factory technical resource","url":"https://www.ridefox.com/tech/bypass-shocks"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The factory Fox shocks on the Gen 1 Raptor are a genuine performance baseline \u2014 but at 50,000+ miles or when you're running the truck hard in the desert, upgrading to a Fox 3.0 or King 2.5 is the single highest-impact suspension dollar you can spend.","description":"Ford's SVT team fitted the Gen 1 Raptor with internal bypass Fox shocks from the factory \u2014 a setup that was genuinely premium for a production truck in 2010. Internal bypass shocks use ports positioned along the shock body that progressively restrict damping as the shock approaches the end of its travel, providing soft compliance in the mid-stroke and firm control at full compression and rebound. It's the same technology used in Baja prerunner builds. The factory tune is competent, but it is a mass-production valving spec \u2014 one tune for every driving style, weight load, and terrain type. An aftermarket 3.0 or 2.5 diameter shock built specifically for the Raptor can be valved for your actual use: loaded overland touring, high-speed desert whoops, or mixed trail and pavement.\n\nThe Fox 3.0 Factory Series front coilovers are the most popular upgrade path. Going from the factory 2.0\" diameter internal bypass to a 3.0\" body doubles the oil volume, which translates directly to heat resistance \u2014 the leading cause of shock fade on a heavily used truck. For desert high-speed driving (the Raptor's core use case), more oil volume means the shocks stay consistent through longer runs. Fox's factory series shocks for the Raptor are pre-valved and ready to install; if you want custom valving, Fox's Racing Series with a custom tune is available at a higher price. King 2.5 OEM Performance is the competing choice: slightly smaller body than the Fox 3.0, but King's reputation for consistency and their excellent warranty make them the preference for some users. RCD's 3.0 coilovers come in at a higher price point but include extended travel provisions if you're also running a RCD lift kit.\n\nThe rear shocks are often overlooked relative to the front, but the Gen 1 Raptor's leaf-spring rear is where the factory tune falls short most noticeably on a loaded overland truck. Upgrading the rear Fox to a 3.0 or King 2.5 provides better high-speed dampening without harsh bottoming when the truck is loaded with camping gear.","steps":["Set the front end at ride height on jack stands before removing anything \u2014 photograph your bumpstop gaps and coilover height for reference during reinstall.","Remove the front wheel and unplug any ABS sensor wire routed near the coilover.","Support the lower control arm with a jack. Remove the upper and lower coilover mounting bolts/nuts.","Remove the factory coilover assembly from the vehicle. If reusing the spring, compress it with a spring compressor and transfer it to the new shock body. If the new coilover ships with a spring, confirm the spring rate matches your intended use \u2014 most Raptor-specific kits include appropriate rates.","Install the new front coilovers and torque to spec (Ford spec: front lower 129 ft-lb, front upper 66 ft-lb \u2014 verify against your specific shock manufacturer's recommended torque as aftermarket mounting hardware may differ).","For rear shocks: support the axle with a jack, remove upper and lower shock bolts, and swap in the new unit. Torque rear lower to 76 ft-lb, rear upper to 66 ft-lb.","Set coilover collar height to match factory ride height before driving. Fine-tune after a 50-mile break-in.","Schedule a front alignment after any suspension work that changes coilover height."],"pros":["Larger shock body (3.0\" vs factory 2.0\") significantly reduces heat fade on hard desert runs","Custom or semi-custom valving options available for specific use cases","Fox and King both have strong warranty and rebuild programs \u2014 these shocks can be serviced rather than replaced","Improved consistency through long travel cycles; notable on back-to-back whoops runs"],"cons":["Expensive \u2014 a full set of Fox 3.0 front and rear is $1,500+ before labor","Requires an alignment after front coilover replacement; budget $100\u2013$150 for that","Does not increase suspension travel on its own \u2014 if you want more travel, you need a full lift kit","Factory Fox shocks at under 50,000 miles are genuinely good; this upgrade is most justified on high-mileage or heavily used trucks"]},{"id":"beadlock-wheel-conversion","title":"Converting to Beadlock-Capable Wheels on the Gen 2 and Gen 3 Raptor","vehicle":"raptor","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"beadlock_wheels","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3.0,"high":6.0},"cost_usd":{"low":1600,"high":3200},"tools_required":["torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","impact wrench","bead seating tool or air compressor"],"skills_required":["wheel and tire mounting","torque sequence knowledge","TPMS service"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2017-2020","2021-present"],"tags":["beadlock","wheels","tires","method","sota","gen2","gen3","airing-down"],"parts":[{"name":"Ford factory forged beadlock-capable 17x8.5 wheel (Gen 2)","vendor":"Ford Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":400},{"name":"Method Race Wheels 105 Beadlock 17x8.5","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":340},{"name":"SOTA Offroad Novakane Beadlock 17x8.5","vendor":"SOTA Offroad","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Beadlock ring hardware kit","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"Method Race Wheels Raptor fitment guide","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com"},{"title":"Ford F-150 Raptor Gen 3 37 Package beadlock wheel specs","url":"https://media.ford.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Gen 2 and Gen 3 Raptor factory forged 17x8.5 wheels are \"beadlock-capable\" \u2014 meaning the wheel accepts a bolt-on beadlock ring. The ring is sold separately. For extreme airing-down (under 10 PSI), beadlocks provide meaningful protection. Most Raptor owners don't need them.","description":"\"Beadlock-capable\" is a specific term. The factory Raptor forged wheels have a machined outer lip that accepts an inner and outer ring system \u2014 you can convert a factory wheel into a true beadlock by purchasing and installing the beadlock ring kit. This is not the same as installing a fully separate aftermarket beadlock wheel, though aftermarket options exist too.\n\nA tire bead is the inner edge of the tire that seals against the wheel rim. Under normal inflation (30\u201345 PSI), the bead is held against the rim by air pressure. If you air down below approximately 8\u201310 PSI, that clamping force decreases enough that aggressive cornering or lateral force can break the bead \u2014 the tire rolls off the rim.\n\nA beadlock clamps the outer tire bead mechanically between the wheel's outer lip and the bolt-on beadlock ring. This mechanical clamping is independent of air pressure. You can run 4\u20135 PSI in sand or rocky terrain without risk of the tire debeading.\n\n**Justified cases:**\n- Regular sand running at 5\u20138 PSI (Arizona dunes, coastal sand, Glamis)\n- Technical rock crawling where tire deformation at low pressure is intentional\n- Competition use\n\n**Cases where beadlocks are overkill:**\n- Desert whoops and high-speed running (Raptor home territory) \u2014 you're running 20\u201325 PSI for speed, not 5 PSI\n- Trail use that doesn't go below 15 PSI\n- Daily driving\n\nMost Raptor owners who want beadlocks are buying them for aesthetics or for occasional hard use. That's a valid choice. But the honest answer is that the truck runs most of its intended mission at 18\u201325 PSI, which doesn't require beadlock clamping.\n\nGen 2 and Gen 3 base Raptors come with factory forged aluminum 17x8.5 wheels. These wheels are machined on the outer lip to accept a beadlock ring from Ford Performance. The ring bolts to the wheel using a series of ring bolts at specific torque values.\n\nThe conversion requires:\n1. Dismount the existing tire from the wheel\n2. Install the beadlock ring inner piece against the wheel\n3. Mount the tire onto the wheel\n4. Install the outer beadlock ring, threading the ring bolts through the ring into the wheel\n5. Torque the ring bolts to spec in a star pattern \u2014 typically 21\u201325 ft-lb depending on the ring hardware\n6. Inflate to seat the inner bead, then final-inflate to operating pressure\n\n**Critical:** Beadlock ring bolts require periodic re-torquing. After the first 100 miles, retorque. Then every 3,000\u20135,000 miles or before any extended trail trip. A loose beadlock ring can cause vibration and, in severe cases, tire failure.\n\n**Method Race Wheels 105 Beadlock** \u2014 well-established in the Raptor community. 17x8.5 in the correct bolt pattern (6x135mm). Forged, strong, proven. $300\u2013$380 per wheel.\n\n**SOTA Offroad Novakane Beadlock** \u2014 more budget-friendly option at $240\u2013$280 per wheel. Heavier than Method but functional. Good choice if you want full beadlock without the full Method price.\n\nWhen switching to aftermarket wheels, verify the hub bore (Ford F-150 is 87.1mm center bore) and confirm the offset. The factory Raptor wheel runs a high positive offset \u2014 going to a significantly different offset changes handling behavior and may affect brake caliper clearance.\n\nFactory TPMS sensors are mounted in the valve stem area and can typically be transferred to aftermarket beadlock wheels. Verify the TPMS sensor mounting style (band-mount vs. valve-stem mount) matches your new wheel's mounting provision. A tire shop can handle the sensor transfer during the tire mounting process.","steps":["Confirm your wheel's bolt pattern (6x135mm, 87.1mm center bore on all Gen 2/3 Raptors)","Choose factory ring kit (for factory wheels) or aftermarket beadlock wheels","Have tires professionally dismounted and remounted with beadlock ring installation","Torque beadlock ring bolts to specification in a star pattern","Check for vibration in the first 50 miles \u2014 ring bolts settling is normal, retorque if vibration develops","Retorque ring bolts at 100 miles, then every 3,000\u20135,000 miles","Transfer TPMS sensors to new wheels before mounting"],"pros":["True tire security at low pressures in sand and rock terrain","Factory forged wheels are convertible without buying new wheels","Method and SOTA offer strong aftermarket options at reasonable prices"],"cons":["Most Raptor use doesn't require beadlocks \u2014 honest assessment","Ring bolts require periodic retorquing \u2014 it's a maintenance item you cannot neglect","Aftermarket wheel offset changes can affect handling \u2014 verify before buying","Added weight per wheel (ring hardware adds 2\u20133 lbs)"]},{"id":"raptor-tire-fitment","title":"What Tires Actually Fit on a Raptor Without a Lift","vehicle":"raptor","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tire_fitment","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":2400},"tools_required":["torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["wheel and tire installation","torque spec adherence"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2014","2017-2020","2021-present"],"tags":["tires","fitment","35s","37s","bfg","ko2","clearance"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich KO2 35x12.50R17","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"BFGoodrich KO2 37x12.50R17","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":390},{"name":"Toyo Open Country AT III 35x12.50R17","vendor":"Toyo","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"Ford F-150 Raptor Gen 2 factory spec \u2014 315/70R17 BFG KO2","url":"https://media.ford.com"},{"title":"Gen 3 Raptor 37 Package specs","url":"https://media.ford.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Factory Gen 2 and Gen 3 base Raptors run 315/70R17 (equivalent to 35x12.5) BFGoodrich KO2s on 17x8.5 wheels. 37s on a Gen 3 require the factory 37 Package or a leveling kit plus minor clearance work.","description":"The Raptor was designed around large tires from day one. Gen 1 SVT ran 315/70R17 BFG All-Terrain T/A KO. Gen 2 moved to the KO2 in the same size. Gen 3 base continues with 315/70R17, and the optional 37 Package steps up to 37x12.5R17.\n\nThe SVT Raptor came from the factory on 315/70R17 BFG All-Terrain T/A KO (later KO2) tires on 17x8.5-inch forged aluminum wheels. This is equivalent to approximately 34.4\" tall \u2014 commonly rounded to 35\" in conversation.\n\nTire swaps on Gen 1: any 315/70R17 fits without modification. Moving to a true 35x12.5R17 adds a small amount of height (roughly 34.4\" vs 35.0\") \u2014 clearance is fine at full stuff with stock suspension in good condition. Rubbing at full lock-to-lock articulation is possible depending on the specific tire brand's width. Test before finalizing.\n\nSame baseline as Gen 1 in concept, upgraded tire. The 315/70R17 KO2 is the OEM tire and the correct size for stock suspension. In practice, the Gen 2's suspension geometry was designed around this tire \u2014 it clears at full compression and full articulation.\n\nRunning true 35x12.5 (slightly taller and wider depending on brand): most 35x12.5R17 tires fit without modification, but the extra section width can cause contact at full compression depending on the tire brand's actual measured width. BFG's 315/70R17 is actually 12.4\" wide; a 35x12.5 from another brand may be slightly wider and may require minor fender liner trimming.\n\n**Running 37s on a Gen 2:** A leveling kit (typically 1.5\"\u20132\") creates enough clearance for 37x12.5R17 tires at full stuff. However, the factory 3.73 gear ratio becomes noticeably strained with 37s. Performance degrades meaningfully \u2014 acceleration, throttle response, and highway cruise RPM all shift unfavorably. If running 37s on a Gen 2, regearing to 4.10 or 4.56 is the right call.\n\nThe Gen 3 diverged into two tire specifications:\n\n**Base Gen 3:** 315/70R17 BFG KO2 \u2014 same as Gen 2, no changes in fitment behavior.\n\n**37 Package (optional):** 37x12.5R17 BFG KO2 on 17x8.5 forged beadlock-capable wheels, paired with 4.10 axle gears and upgraded Brembo brakes. This is a factory-engineered package \u2014 Ford sized the fenders, calibrated the ABS, and chose the gears specifically for 37s. It is not an afterthought.\n\nRunning 37s on a non-37-Package Gen 3: technically possible with a leveling kit, but you're missing the 4.10 gears that make the truck functional with the taller tires. The non-37-Package Gen 3 has 3.73 gears \u2014 the same penalty as Gen 2 with 37s.\n\nAt full suspension compression (both wheels droop), the Raptor's long-travel suspension moves the tire significantly upward relative to the body. Factory 315/70R17 clears in all four corners without issue. The concern zone is the front at full compression combined with full steering lock. On stock suspension with stock 315/70R17, clearance is designed in. Adding width (wider tire brand) or height (37s without a leveling kit) changes this.\n\nA practical test: with the truck on flat ground, turn the wheel to full lock in both directions and bounce the front of the truck manually. If it rubs, you need more clearance before going off-road where actual full-compression events will happen.","steps":["Confirm your generation and whether Gen 3 has the 37 Package (window sticker or door jamb)","For same-size replacements (315/70R17): any quality AT or MT tire in that size bolts on without modification","For 35x12.5 on Gen 1 or Gen 2: check the specific brand's measured section width before ordering \u2014 test for rub at full lock before trail use","For 37s on Gen 2 or non-37-Package Gen 3: plan for regearing to 4.10 or 4.56 and minor fender liner trimming","Torque lug nuts to factory spec (150 ft-lb on Gen 2, 150 ft-lb on Gen 3)","Reset TPMS after any wheel/tire change"],"pros":["315/70R17 is widely available from multiple quality manufacturers","37s on a factory 37-Package Gen 3 are properly integrated \u2014 no compromises","The 17-inch wheel size keeps tire costs lower than 20-inch equivalents"],"cons":["37s without the 4.10 gear package are a real performance compromise","Gen 2 with 37s needs regearing \u2014 that's $2,500\u2013$4,000 in additional work","Some 35x12.5 tires are genuinely wider than the factory 315/70 \u2014 verify before buying"]}];